{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Do not fear.</b> It has no resh-ayin root (ריע, 'friend'); and the substitution with alef is not correct in this place, because of the missing yod from the maktev, since the vav is a root letter." "And the meaning of <b>do not fear</b>: when these troubles come upon Babylon, do not fear, you (these are they)." "<b>Have I not from of old</b> made known to you, <b>and you are My witnesses</b> — and behold, you see: <b>Is there an elohim besides Me? And there is no Rock [I did not know]</b> — that I did not know him, for he is emptiness and vanity [and waste]. And some say it is like the sense of 'and he knew the men of Succoth' (Judges 8:16). But the correct interpretation is a hint to the multitude of [one's] virtues."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra argues against reading תרהו as from ראה (with alef) since the vav in the form would have to be a root letter, requiring a yod in the root spelling.", 2 "The bracketed [ובוהו] appears in some witnesses as a variant reading; [בל ידעתי] is the lemma from the verse being cited."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:57:58.159981790Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}